    shortDescription: "Explain value proposition.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA value proposition clearly states how our solution solves a customer's problem and delivers benefits. I articulate it by linking our capabilities to their specific pain points and desired outcomes, using quantitative evidence. In an interview, I would explain this by connecting the concept to the real PreSales workflow. The important point is not only what the concept means, but how I would apply it with a customer, how I would make decisions, what risks I would consider, and what outcome I would expect. I would begin with the customer's business objective and constraints, then translate the requirement into a practical technical or commercial approach. If several approaches are possible, I would compare them using business value, technical feasibility, cost, risk, timeline, security, scalability, maintainability, and customer impact rather than choosing an option only because it has.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is asked to see whether you can turn a customer requirement into a technically credible recommendation. The interviewer is looking for assumptions, trade-offs, validation steps, and a clear link between business need and solution.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A value proposition clearly states how our solution solves a customer's problem and delivers benefits.\n2. I articulate it by linking our capabilities to their specific pain points and desired outcomes, using quantitative evidence.\n3. In an interview, I would explain this by connecting the concept to the real PreSales workflow.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a customer solution discussion, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A value proposition clearly states how our solution solves a customer's problem and delivers benefits.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn pre-sales, the concept matters when matching a customer requirement to a technically credible solution. The recommendation should state assumptions, integration constraints, risks, and what would need to be validated in a proof of concept.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Applying a process without considering the actual role, customer, policy, or business context.\n- Making a decision without documenting the relevant evidence or assumptions.\n- Using an absolute rule where the facts of the case should drive the decision.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
