    focus: null,
    question: "What is the difference between a POC and a pilot?",
    slug: "poc-vs-pilot",
    shortDescription: "Compare POC and pilot.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA POC is a technical feasibility test, often shorter and limited to a small team. A pilot is a broader, production-like deployment to a limited user group, often used to validate operations and user acceptance. In an interview, I would explain this by connecting the concept to the real PreSales workflow. The important point is not only what the concept means, but how I would apply it with a customer, how I would make decisions, what risks I would consider, and what outcome I would expect. I would begin with the customer's business objective and constraints, then translate the requirement into a practical technical or commercial approach. If several approaches are possible, I would compare them using business value, technical feasibility, cost, risk, timeline, security, scalability, maintainability, and customer impact rather than choosing an option only because it has more features. I.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is asked to see whether you can turn a customer requirement into a technically credible recommendation. The interviewer is looking for assumptions, trade-offs, validation steps, and a clear link between business need and solution.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A POC is a technical feasibility test, often shorter and limited to a small team.\n2. A pilot is a broader, production-like deployment to a limited user group, often used to validate operations and user acceptance.\n3. In an interview, I would explain this by connecting the concept to the real PreSales workflow.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a customer solution discussion, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A POC is a technical feasibility test, often shorter and limited to a small team.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn pre-sales, the concept matters when matching a customer requirement to a technically credible solution. The recommendation should state assumptions, integration constraints, risks, and what would need to be validated in a proof of concept.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA POC is a technical feasibility test, often shorter and limited to a small team. A pilot is a broader, production-like deployment to a limited user group, often used to validate operations and user acceptance. In an interview, I would explain this by connecting the concept to the real PreSales workflow. The important point is not only what the concept means, but how I would apply it with a customer, how I would make decisions, what risks I would consider, and what outcome I would expect. I would begin with the customer's business objective and constraints, then translate the requirement into a practical technical or commercial approach. If several approaches are possible, I would compare them using business value, technical feasibility, cost, risk, timeline, security, scalability, maintainability, and customer impact rather than choosing an option only because it has more features. I.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is asked to see whether you can turn a customer requirement into a technically credible recommendation. The interviewer is looking for assumptions, trade-offs, validation steps, and a clear link between business need and solution.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A POC is a technical feasibility test, often shorter and limited to a small team.\n2. A pilot is a broader, production-like deployment to a limited user group, often used to validate operations and user acceptance.\n3. In an interview, I would explain this by connecting the concept to the real PreSales workflow.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a customer solution discussion, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A POC is a technical feasibility test, often shorter and limited to a small team.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn pre-sales, the concept matters when matching a customer requirement to a technically credible solution. The recommendation should state assumptions, integration constraints, risks, and what would need to be validated in a proof of concept.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
