    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI stay motivated by focusing on the customer's success and the value we bring. I break the cycle into milestones, celebrate small wins, and collaborate with my team for support. I also take breaks to recharge. In an interview, I would explain this by connecting the concept to the real PreSales workflow. The important point is not only what the concept means, but how I would apply it with a customer, how I would make decisions, what risks I would consider, and what outcome I would expect. I would begin with the customer's business objective and constraints, then translate the requirement into a practical technical or commercial approach. If several approaches are possible, I would compare them using business value, technical feasibility, cost, risk, timeline, security, scalability, maintainability, and customer impact rather than choosing an option only because it has more features. I.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is asked to see whether you can turn a customer requirement into a technically credible recommendation. The interviewer is looking for assumptions, trade-offs, validation steps, and a clear link between business need and solution.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. I stay motivated by focusing on the customer's success and the value we bring.\n2. I break the cycle into milestones, celebrate small wins, and collaborate with my team for support.\n3. I also take breaks to recharge.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a customer solution discussion can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. I stay motivated by focusing on the customer's success and the value we bring.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn pre-sales, the concept matters when matching a customer requirement to a technically credible solution. The recommendation should state assumptions, integration constraints, risks, and what would need to be validated in a proof of concept.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
