    slug: "python2-vs-python3",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two major versions.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPython 3 is the future; Python 2 reached end-of-life in 2020. Key differences: print is a function in Python 3 (`print()`), integer division returns float in Python 3 (`/`), Unicode strings by default, range() returns an iterator (not list), and many syntax changes (except as, metaclasses). Always use Python 3 for new projects.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between python 2 and python 3 and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Python 3 is the future; Python 2 reached end-of-life in 2020.\n2. Key differences: print is a function in Python 3 (`print()`), integer division returns float in Python 3 (`/`), Unicode strings by default, range() returns an iterator (not list), and many syntax changes (except as, metaclasses).\n3. Always use Python 3 for new projects.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Python service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Python 3 is the future; Python 2 reached end-of-life in 2020.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Python service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Python 3 is the future; Python 2 reached end-of-life in 2020.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
