    slug: "python-interpreter",
    shortDescription: "Explain the execution model.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPython is an interpreted language: source code is compiled to bytecode (.pyc) which is then executed by the Python Virtual Machine (PVM). The interpreter (CPython) reads, tokenizes, parses, compiles, and runs the bytecode. There are also alternative implementations like PyPy (JIT), Jython (Java), and IronPython (.NET).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how is python interpreted, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Python is an interpreted language: source code is compiled to bytecode (.pyc) which is then executed by the Python Virtual Machine (PVM).\n2. The interpreter (CPython) reads, tokenizes, parses, compiles, and runs the bytecode.\n3. There are also alternative implementations like PyPy (JIT), Jython (Java), and IronPython (.NET).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a Python service can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Python is an interpreted language: source code is compiled to bytecode (.pyc) which is then executed by the Python Virtual Machine (PVM).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production codebase, how is python interpreted matters when maintainability, correctness, performance, or user behavior is affected by the choice. The implementation should be tested against the actual path rather than only an isolated example.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
