    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLists are mutable (can be modified after creation), tuples are immutable. Lists use square brackets `[]`, tuples use parentheses `()`. Tuples are generally faster and can be used as dictionary keys; lists cannot. Use tuples for fixed data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a list and a tuple and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Lists are mutable (can be modified after creation), tuples are immutable.\n2. Lists use square brackets `[]`, tuples use parentheses `()`.\n3. Tuples are generally faster and can be used as dictionary keys; lists cannot.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Python service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Lists are mutable (can be modified after creation), tuples are immutable.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Python service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Lists are mutable (can be modified after creation), tuples are immutable.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
