    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTuples are smaller and faster than lists because they are immutable and have less overhead. Lists allocate extra capacity for future growth; tuples allocate exactly the required space.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `tuple` and `list` in terms of memory and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Tuples are smaller and faster than lists because they are immutable and have less overhead.\n2. Lists allocate extra capacity for future growth; tuples allocate exactly the required space.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Python service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Tuples are smaller and faster than lists because they are immutable and have less overhead.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Python service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Tuples are smaller and faster than lists because they are immutable and have less overhead.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
