    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nShallow copy creates a new container object but populates it with references to the same elements as the original. Deep copy recursively copies all nested objects, creating completely independent copies. Use `copy.copy()` and `copy.deepcopy()`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between shallow copy and deep copy and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Shallow copy creates a new container object but populates it with references to the same elements as the original.\n2. Deep copy recursively copies all nested objects, creating completely independent copies.\n3. Use `copy.copy()` and `copy.deepcopy()`.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Python service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Shallow copy creates a new container object but populates it with references to the same elements as the original.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Python service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Shallow copy creates a new container object but populates it with references to the same elements as the original.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
