    shortDescription: "Compare module and package.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA module is a single Python file (`.py`). A package is a directory containing an `__init__.py` file (or namespace package) and multiple modules/subpackages. Packages allow hierarchical structuring.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a module and a package and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A module is a single Python file (`.py`).\n2. A package is a directory containing an `__init__.py` file (or namespace package) and multiple modules/subpackages.\n3. Packages allow hierarchical structuring.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Python service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A module is a single Python file (`.py`).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Python service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A module is a single Python file (`.py`).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
