    slug: "except-vs-except-exception",
    shortDescription: "Compare exception catching.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`except:` catches all exceptions (including `KeyboardInterrupt` and `SystemExit`). `except Exception as e` catches only exceptions derived from `Exception` (excluding system exits). It's better to catch specific exceptions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `except` and `except exception as e` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `except:` catches all exceptions (including `KeyboardInterrupt` and `SystemExit`).\n2. `except Exception as e` catches only exceptions derived from `Exception` (excluding system exits).\n3. It's better to catch specific exceptions.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Python service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `except:` catches all exceptions (including `KeyboardInterrupt` and `SystemExit`).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Python service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `except:` catches all exceptions (including `KeyboardInterrupt` and `SystemExit`).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
