    slug: "lock-vs-rlock",
    shortDescription: "Compare locks.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`Lock` can be acquired once. `RLock` (reentrant lock) can be acquired multiple times by the same thread without deadlock. Use `RLock` when a thread may need to acquire the same lock again.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `lock` and `rlock` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `Lock` can be acquired once.\n2. `RLock` (reentrant lock) can be acquired multiple times by the same thread without deadlock.\n3. Use `RLock` when a thread may need to acquire the same lock again.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Python service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `Lock` can be acquired once.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Python service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `Lock` can be acquired once.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
