    question: "What is Quality Assurance (QA)?",
    slug: "what-is-quality-assurance-qa",
    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is quality assurance (qa) with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQuality Assurance is the broader discipline of building and improving processes, practices, and controls that help a team produce quality software consistently. QA includes testing, but also requirement reviews, process improvement, standards, defect prevention, risk management, metrics, and continuous learning. A useful distinction is that testing mainly evaluates the product, while QA also asks why defects entered the product and how the development process can prevent similar problems. In modern teams, QA is a shared responsibility across product, development, testing, operations, and security.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Quality Assurance is the broader discipline of building and improving processes, practices, and controls that help a team produce quality software consistently.\n2. QA includes testing, but also requirement reviews, process improvement, standards, defect prevention, risk management, metrics, and continuous learning.\n3. A useful distinction is that testing mainly evaluates the product, while QA also asks why defects entered the product and how the development process can prevent similar problems.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Quality Assurance is the broader discipline of building and improving processes, practices, and controls that help a team produce quality software consistently.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
