    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is quality control (qc) with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQuality Control focuses on evaluating the actual product or deliverable to identify whether it meets defined requirements and quality expectations. Testing, inspection, measurement, and defect reporting are common QC activities. QA is generally broader and more preventive, while QC is more product-focused and detection-oriented, although organizations may define the terms differently. In an interview, I would emphasize that both work together: QA improves the process and QC provides evidence about the resulting product.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Quality Control focuses on evaluating the actual product or deliverable to identify whether it meets defined requirements and quality expectations.\n2. Testing, inspection, measurement, and defect reporting are common QC activities.\n3. QA is generally broader and more preventive, while QC is more product-focused and detection-oriented, although organizations may define the terms differently.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Quality Control focuses on evaluating the actual product or deliverable to identify whether it meets defined requirements and quality expectations.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
