    company: null,
    companySlug: null,
    focus: null,
    question: "What is the difference between QA and testing?",
    slug: "what-is-the-difference-between-qa-and-testing",
    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is the difference between qa and testing with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQA is a broader quality discipline covering prevention, process improvement, standards, risk management, and quality practices across the lifecycle. Testing is one QA activity that evaluates software and attempts to expose failures. For example, reviewing acceptance criteria for ambiguity is a preventive QA activity, while executing a checkout scenario and reporting an incorrect total is testing. A QA engineer may perform both, but quality ownership should extend beyond executing test cases.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. QA is a broader quality discipline covering prevention, process improvement, standards, risk management, and quality practices across the lifecycle.\n2. Testing is one QA activity that evaluates software and attempts to expose failures.\n3. For example, reviewing acceptance criteria for ambiguity is a preventive QA activity, while executing a checkout scenario and reporting an incorrect total is testing.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. QA is a broader quality discipline covering prevention, process improvement, standards, risk management, and quality practices across the lifecycle.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
