    shortDescription: "Interview question on what are the seven principles of testing with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe seven commonly taught principles are: testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence; exhaustive testing is impossible; early testing saves time and cost; defects tend to cluster; repeating the same tests can lose effectiveness, known as the pesticide paradox; testing is context dependent; and absence-of-errors is a fallacy because software can satisfy technical checks while still failing user or business needs. These principles guide prioritization and test design rather than acting as rigid rules.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The seven commonly taught principles are: testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence; exhaustive testing is impossible; early testing saves time and cost; defects tend to cluster; repeating the same tests can lose effectiveness, known as the pesticide paradox; testing is context dependent; and absence-of-errors is a fallacy because software can satisfy technical checks while still failing user or business needs.\n2. These principles guide prioritization and test design rather than acting as rigid rules.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The seven commonly taught principles are: testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence; exhaustive testing is impossible; early testing saves time and cost; defects tend to cluster; repeating the same tests can lose effectiveness, known as the pesticide paradox; testing is context dependent; and absence-of-errors is a fallacy because software can satisfy technical checks while still failing user or business needs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
