    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is sdlc with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe Software Development Life Cycle is the overall set of activities used to plan, build, test, release, operate, and maintain software. Traditional models may describe separate phases, while Agile and DevOps perform many activities continuously. QA contributes throughout the lifecycle by clarifying requirements, identifying risks early, testing increments, validating integrations, and learning from production behavior. The important interview point is that quality should be built into the lifecycle rather than postponed until the end.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The Software Development Life Cycle is the overall set of activities used to plan, build, test, release, operate, and maintain software.\n2. Traditional models may describe separate phases, while Agile and DevOps perform many activities continuously.\n3. QA contributes throughout the lifecycle by clarifying requirements, identifying risks early, testing increments, validating integrations, and learning from production behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The Software Development Life Cycle is the overall set of activities used to plan, build, test, release, operate, and maintain software.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
