    question: "What is Shift Left testing?",
    slug: "what-is-shift-left-testing",
    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is shift left testing with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nShift Left means moving suitable quality activities earlier in the lifecycle so defects and misunderstandings are found when they are cheaper to address. Examples include requirement reviews, acceptance-criteria examples, design reviews, static analysis, unit tests, contract checks, and early automation. Shift Left does not eliminate later system, exploratory, security, performance, or production testing because some risks can only be evaluated in integrated or real environments.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Shift Left means moving suitable quality activities earlier in the lifecycle so defects and misunderstandings are found when they are cheaper to address.\n2. Examples include requirement reviews, acceptance-criteria examples, design reviews, static analysis, unit tests, contract checks, and early automation.\n3. Shift Left does not eliminate later system, exploratory, security, performance, or production testing because some risks can only be evaluated in integrated or real environments.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Shift Left means moving suitable quality activities earlier in the lifecycle so defects and misunderstandings are found when they are cheaper to address.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
