    question: "How do you test a user story?",
    slug: "how-do-you-test-a-user-story",
    shortDescription: "Interview question on how do you test a user story with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would approach test a user story as a risk-based QA problem. The practical focus is collaboration and fast feedback. QA should participate in refinement, clarify acceptance criteria, identify negative and edge scenarios, prepare test data, test continuously, and help the team understand release risk. Agile does not mean skipping documentation or exploratory testing, and automation is valuable only when it provides reliable feedback. Quality should be part of the team's shared Definition of Done rather than being postponed to the end of a sprint. I would document assumptions, use representative data, verify the expected result independently, and preserve evidence for any important failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. I would approach test a user story as a risk-based QA problem.\n2. The practical focus is collaboration and fast feedback.\n3. QA should participate in refinement, clarify acceptance criteria, identify negative and edge scenarios, prepare test data, test continuously, and help the team understand release risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. I would approach test a user story as a risk-based QA problem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
