    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSanity testing is a focused check around a particular change, defect fix, or small area of functionality to determine whether the change behaves as expected and whether closely related behavior is still reasonable. Teams use the term differently, so I would follow the project's agreed definition. The key distinction is scope: sanity testing is targeted, while regression testing considers a broader set of existing behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Sanity testing is a focused check around a particular change, defect fix, or small area of functionality to determine whether the change behaves as expected and whether closely related behavior is still reasonable.\n2. Teams use the term differently, so I would follow the project's agreed definition.\n3. The key distinction is scope: sanity testing is targeted, while regression testing considers a broader set of existing behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Sanity testing is a focused check around a particular change, defect fix, or small area of functionality to determine whether the change behaves as expected and whether closely related behavior is still reasonable.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
