    question: "What is regression testing?",
    slug: "what-is-regression-testing",
    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is regression testing with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRegression testing checks that existing behavior has not been unintentionally broken by new or changed software. I select regression scope based on changed areas, dependencies, defect history, business criticality, and risk rather than automatically running every test in every situation. Fast automated checks are valuable for stable behavior, while exploratory and targeted manual testing remain useful for areas that require judgment or change frequently. A regression suite must also be maintained to remain trustworthy.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Regression testing checks that existing behavior has not been unintentionally broken by new or changed software.\n2. I select regression scope based on changed areas, dependencies, defect history, business criticality, and risk rather than automatically running every test in every situation.\n3. Fast automated checks are valuable for stable behavior, while exploratory and targeted manual testing remain useful for areas that require judgment or change frequently.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Regression testing checks that existing behavior has not been unintentionally broken by new or changed software.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
