    question: "What is a test case?",
    slug: "what-is-a-test-case",
    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is a test case with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA test case is a defined set of preconditions, inputs, actions, and expected results used to evaluate a particular behavior or condition. A good test case is understandable, traceable when traceability matters, repeatable when repeatability is required, and precise enough to produce a meaningful result. It should include relevant test data and cleanup expectations. Not every test needs a formal case; exploratory testing can use a charter while still requiring a clear objective and evidence.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A test case is a defined set of preconditions, inputs, actions, and expected results used to evaluate a particular behavior or condition.\n2. A good test case is understandable, traceable when traceability matters, repeatable when repeatability is required, and precise enough to produce a meaningful result.\n3. It should include relevant test data and cleanup expectations.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A test case is a defined set of preconditions, inputs, actions, and expected results used to evaluate a particular behavior or condition.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
