    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would approach avoid duplicate test cases as a risk-based QA problem. Good test assets make intent, data, expected results, and traceability clear enough to support reliable execution. I design data around business rules, boundaries, relationships, permissions, and failure conditions, while protecting sensitive information. I also keep test cases maintainable: obsolete cases should be removed or updated, duplicate coverage should be consolidated, and high-risk scenarios should be easy to identify. Test documentation should be proportional to risk and team needs. I would document assumptions, use representative data, verify the expected result independently, and preserve evidence for any important failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. I would approach avoid duplicate test cases as a risk-based QA problem.\n2. Good test assets make intent, data, expected results, and traceability clear enough to support reliable execution.\n3. I design data around business rules, boundaries, relationships, permissions, and failure conditions, while protecting sensitive information.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. I would approach avoid duplicate test cases as a risk-based QA problem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
