    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhat is severity vs priority is a QA concept used to evaluate, prevent, or manage software quality. Defect management is about creating a reliable flow from observation to decision and learning. A useful defect report contains reproducible evidence, expected versus actual behavior, impact, environment, and relevant diagnostics. Severity and priority are separate dimensions. After a fix, I confirm the original problem and perform appropriate regression. At a mature level, I also analyze escaped defects and root causes to improve prevention rather than merely increasing the number of reported bugs. A strong answer connects the concept to an observable example and explains when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. What is severity vs priority is a QA concept used to evaluate, prevent, or manage software quality.\n2. Defect management is about creating a reliable flow from observation to decision and learning.\n3. A useful defect report contains reproducible evidence, expected versus actual behavior, impact, environment, and relevant diagnostics.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. What is severity vs priority is a QA concept used to evaluate, prevent, or manage software quality.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
