    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is api testing with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAPI testing evaluates application interfaces directly by validating requests, responses, business rules, side effects, security, and error behavior. It can cover REST, GraphQL, SOAP, gRPC, messaging, and other interfaces. I validate schemas and status codes but also check authentication, authorization, boundary values, idempotency, persistence, emitted events, and dependency failures. API tests are often a high-value automation layer because they provide strong feedback without the cost and fragility of full UI workflows.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. API testing evaluates application interfaces directly by validating requests, responses, business rules, side effects, security, and error behavior.\n2. It can cover REST, GraphQL, SOAP, gRPC, messaging, and other interfaces.\n3. I validate schemas and status codes but also check authentication, authorization, boundary values, idempotency, persistence, emitted events, and dependency failures.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. API testing evaluates application interfaces directly by validating requests, responses, business rules, side effects, security, and error behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
