    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would approach test a rest api as a risk-based QA problem. API testing should start from the contract and business behavior rather than only checking that an HTTP response is returned. I validate request and response schemas, status codes, headers, authentication, authorization, negative cases, boundaries, side effects, idempotency, compatibility, and dependency failures. API tests are often a high-value automation layer because they are faster and less presentation-dependent than UI tests. I would document assumptions, use representative data, verify the expected result independently, and preserve evidence for any important failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. I would approach test a rest api as a risk-based QA problem.\n2. API testing should start from the contract and business behavior rather than only checking that an HTTP response is returned.\n3. I validate request and response schemas, status codes, headers, authentication, authorization, negative cases, boundaries, side effects, idempotency, compatibility, and dependency failures.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. I would approach test a rest api as a risk-based QA problem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would approach test a rest api as a risk-based QA problem. API testing should start from the contract and business behavior rather than only checking that an HTTP response is returned. I validate request and response schemas, status codes, headers, authentication, authorization, negative cases, boundaries, side effects, idempotency, compatibility, and dependency failures. API tests are often a high-value automation layer because they are faster and less presentation-dependent than UI tests. I would document assumptions, use representative data, verify the expected result independently, and preserve evidence for any important failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. I would approach test a rest api as a risk-based QA problem.\n2. API testing should start from the contract and business behavior rather than only checking that an HTTP response is returned.\n3. I validate request and response schemas, status codes, headers, authentication, authorization, negative cases, boundaries, side effects, idempotency, compatibility, and dependency failures.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. I would approach test a rest api as a risk-based QA problem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
