    question: "What is performance testing?",
    slug: "what-is-performance-testing",
    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is performance testing with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPerformance testing evaluates how software behaves under defined workloads, including response time, throughput, concurrency, resource utilization, scalability, and stability. I define the workload and measurable acceptance criteria first, then monitor application and infrastructure signals together. Different tests answer different questions: load testing checks expected demand, stress testing explores limits, spike testing examines sudden changes, and soak testing evaluates long-running stability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Performance testing evaluates how software behaves under defined workloads, including response time, throughput, concurrency, resource utilization, scalability, and stability.\n2. I define the workload and measurable acceptance criteria first, then monitor application and infrastructure signals together.\n3. Different tests answer different questions: load testing checks expected demand, stress testing explores limits, spike testing examines sudden changes, and soak testing evaluates long-running stability.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Performance testing evaluates how software behaves under defined workloads, including response time, throughput, concurrency, resource utilization, scalability, and stability.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
