    shortDescription: "Interview question on what is security testing with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSecurity testing evaluates whether software protects data, identities, access, and operations according to its security requirements and threat model. QA can test authentication, authorization, session handling, input validation, sensitive-data exposure, rate limits, and secure error behavior, while specialized security teams may perform penetration testing. The most important principle is to verify security controls at the server or trust boundary rather than relying on UI restrictions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Security testing evaluates whether software protects data, identities, access, and operations according to its security requirements and threat model.\n2. QA can test authentication, authorization, session handling, input validation, sensitive-data exposure, rate limits, and secure error behavior, while specialized security teams may perform penetration testing.\n3. The most important principle is to verify security controls at the server or trust boundary rather than relying on UI restrictions.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can apply the control to an actual request or resource boundary and verify that an unauthorized action is rejected. Security testing evaluates whether software protects data, identities, access, and operations according to its security requirements and threat model.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
